---
/**
 * English SRL page. Not a translation of the Romanian one — the audience is
 * different, so the content is too.
 *
 * A Romanian reader wants to know what the monthly service includes. A
 * foreign owner mostly wants to know how the Romanian system differs from
 * the one they know: what a microîntreprindere is, why VAT registration is
 * not automatic, and who is liable if a filing is late. "Ploiești" stays out
 * of the title and H1 here — an English searcher looking for this is
 * searching by country, not by city.
 */
import ServiceLayout from '@layouts/ServiceLayout.astro';
import Tbd from '@components/ui/Tbd.astro';
---

<ServiceLayout service="srl" serviceType="Financial accounting">
  <p class="lede">
    We keep the books for Romanian limited companies (SRL) in Ploiești and Prahova,
    including foreign-owned ones. Filings go to the Romanian tax authority in Romanian;
    your reporting and our correspondence with you are in English.
  </p>

  <p>
    Most owners who come to us from another country are not looking for a different
    service — they are looking to understand what the Romanian system requires of them.
    The sections below cover the parts that usually differ from what people expect.
  </p>

  <h2>What the monthly service covers</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Recording of all source documents: sales and purchase invoices, receipts, bank statements, expense claims</li>
    <li>Statutory registers — journal, inventory register and general ledger</li>
    <li>Corporate income tax, or micro-company turnover tax, depending on which regime applies</li>
    <li>Fixed asset register and depreciation</li>
    <li>Filing through SPV, the tax authority's electronic portal</li>
    <li>A monthly trial balance and a short statement of what is payable and when</li>
    <li>Handling notices and information requests from ANAF</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Two tax regimes, and the choice is not permanent</h2>

  <p>
    A Romanian SRL is taxed either as a micro-company, on turnover, or at the standard
    corporate income tax rate on profit. The micro-company regime applies below a
    turnover threshold of <Tbd label="micro-company turnover threshold" /> and has
    conditions attached — including <Tbd label="micro-company conditions, e.g. employee requirement" />.
  </p>

  <p>
    A company that exceeds the threshold moves to profit taxation from
    <Tbd label="point at which the regime changes" />. This is worth modelling before
    incorporation rather than after: the two regimes produce materially different tax
    bills at the same revenue, and the more profitable the company, the more the
    difference matters.
  </p>

  <h2>VAT registration is separate from incorporation</h2>

  <p>
    Registering a company does not register it for VAT. A new SRL starts outside the VAT
    system and must register once turnover passes <Tbd label="VAT registration threshold" />,
    within <Tbd label="deadline to register after exceeding threshold" /> of exceeding it.
    Miss that and VAT is owed retroactively from the date the threshold was crossed, plus
    <Tbd label="late registration penalties" />.
  </p>

  <p>
    Voluntary registration before the threshold is possible and is often the right call
    for a company that buys heavily from Romanian VAT-registered suppliers, or that sells
    business-to-business. If your company trades within the EU, registration also brings
    the recapitulative statement and an EU VAT number into scope.
  </p>

  <h2>The director is personally exposed</h2>

  <p>
    Under Romanian law the administrator carries personal responsibility for the
    company's tax obligations being met, and that responsibility does not transfer to the
    accountant. It is the single point most foreign owners are surprised by. In practice
    it means two things: filings must go in on time even in a month when the company did
    nothing, and you should be able to see what was filed on your behalf.
  </p>

  <p>
    We send a monthly summary of what was submitted and what was paid, in English, so
    there is a record you can actually read.
  </p>

  <h2>Electronic invoicing is mandatory</h2>

  <p>
    Romania requires invoices to be issued through RO e-Factura, the national e-invoicing
    system, and received through SPV. This is not optional and applies to foreign-owned
    companies on the same terms. It is explained in full on our
    <a href="/en/services/e-invoicing-spv">e-Factura and SPV page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Taking over from your current accountant</h2>

  <p>
    We take over in <Tbd label="handover duration" /> without interrupting trading. We
    request the trial balance, the statutory registers and the last filed annual accounts
    from your current accountant, reconcile the opening balances and continue from there.
    You will need to grant us access in SPV or issue a power of attorney for filing.
  </p>

  <h2>Fees</h2>

  <p>
    From <Tbd label="monthly fee, non-VAT-registered" /> per month for a company outside
    the VAT system and from <Tbd label="monthly fee, VAT-registered" /> per month for a
    VAT-registered one. The figure depends on document volume, headcount and the number
    of bank accounts. We quote a firm price after seeing the volume, not before.
  </p>
</ServiceLayout>
